window.lectureSlides = [{"hidden":false,"text":"The E/R Model\n1","title":"The E/R Model","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"E/R is the bridge between requirements and tables\nRequirements describe the world: people, objects, events, and rules.\nThe E/R model makes the meaning visible: entities, relationships, keys, and constraints.\nThe relational schema implements that meaning: tables, primary keys, and foreign keys.\n2","title":"E/R is the bridge between requirements and tables","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. E/R Basics: Entities & Relations\n3","title":"1. E/R Basics: Entities & Relations","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Database Design\nDatabase design: Why do we need it?\nAgree on structure of the database before deciding on a particular implementation\nConsider issues such as:\nWhat entities to model\nHow entities are related\nWhat constraints exist in the domain\nHow to achieve good designs\nSeveral formalisms exist\nWe discuss one flavor of E/R diagrams\n4","title":"Database Design","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Database Design Process\nRequirements analysis\nWhat is going to be stored?\nHow is it going to be used?\nWhat are we going to do with the data?\nWho should access the data?\nTechnical and non-technical people are involved\n1. Requirements Analysis\n2. Conceptual Design\n3. Logical, Physical, Security, etc.\n5","title":"Database Design Process","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Database Design Process\n2. Conceptual Design\nA high-level description of the database\nSufficiently precise that technical people can understand it\nBut, not so precise that non-technical people can’t participate\nThis is where E/R fits in.\n1. Requirements Analysis\n2. Conceptual Design\n3. Logical, Physical, Security, etc.\n6","title":"Database Design Process","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Database Design Process\n1. Requirements Analysis\n2. Conceptual Design\n3. Logical, Physical, Security, etc.\n3. More:\nLogical Database Design\nPhysical Database Design\nSecurity Design\n7","title":"Database Design Process","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"This process is iterated many times\nE/R is a visual syntax for DB design which is precise enough for technical points, but abstracted enough for non-technical people\nDatabase Design Process\n8\n1. Requirements Analysis\n2. Conceptual Design\n3. Logical, Physical, Security, etc.\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nE/R Model & Diagrams used","title":"This process is iterated ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Interlude: Impact of the ER model\nThe E/R model is one of the most cited articles in Computer Science\n“The Entity-Relationship model – toward a unified view of data” Peter Chen, 1976\nUsed by companies big and small\nYou’ll know it soon enough\n9","title":"Interlude: Impact of the ER model","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"This process is iterated many times\nE/R is a visual syntax for DB design which is precise enough for technical points, but abstracted enough for non-technical people\nDatabase Design Process\n1. Requirements Analysis\n2. Conceptual Design\n3. Logical, Physical, Security, etc.\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nE/R Model & Diagrams used\n10","title":"This process is iterated ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Turn requirement sentences into a model\nNouns suggest entities: Student, Course, Instructor.\nVerbs suggest relationships: enrolls in, teaches, advises.\nAdjectives and measurements suggest attributes: title, credits, start date.\nWords such as every, at most, and must reveal constraints.\n11","title":"Turn requirement sentences into a model","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Interlude: Impact of the ER model\nThe E/R model is one of the most cited articles in Computer Science\n“The Entity-Relationship model – toward a unified view of data” Peter Chen, 1976\nUsed by companies big and small\nYou’ll know it soon enough\n12","title":"Interlude: Impact of the ER model","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Entities and Entity Sets\nEntities & entity sets are the primitive unit of the E/R model\nEntities are the individual objects, which are members of entity sets\nEx: A specific person or product\nEntity sets are the classes or types of objects in our model\nEx: Person, Product\nThese are what is shown in E/R diagrams - as rectangles\nEntity sets represent the sets of all possible entities\nProduct\nPerson\nThese represent entity sets\n13","title":"Entities and Entity Sets","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Entities and Entity Sets\nAn entity set has attributes\nRepresented by ovals attached to an entity set\n14\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nShapes are important. Colors are not.\nA nice drawing tool is ERdoc Playground","title":"Entities and Entity Sets","width":1440,"height":855,"links":["https://erdoc.dcc.uchile.cl/"]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Entities vs. Entity Sets\nExample:\n15\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nEntity Set\nProduct\nName: Xbox\nCategory: Total Multimedia System\nPrice: $250\nName: My Little Pony Doll\nCategory: Toy\nPrice: $25\nEntity\nEntity Attribute\nEntities are not explicitly represented in E/R diagrams!","title":"Entities vs. Entity Sets","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Keys\nA key is a minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies an entity.\n16\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nDenote elements of the primary key by underlining.\nHere, {name, category} is not a key (it is not minimal).\nIf it were, what would it mean?\nThe E/R model forces us to designate a single primary key, though there may be multiple candidate keys","title":"Keys","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Multivalued and Derived Attributes\n17\nMultivalued Attribute\nDerived Attribute","title":"Multivalued and Derived Attributes","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"The R in E/R: Relationships\nA relationship is between two entities\n18\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nMakes","title":"The R in E/R: ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Entities and Entity Sets\nAn entity set has attributes\nRepresented by ovals attached to an entity set\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nShapes are important. Colors are not.\n19\nA nice drawing tool is ERdoc Playground","title":"Entities and Entity Sets","width":1440,"height":855,"links":["https://erdoc.dcc.uchile.cl/"]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Entities vs. Entity Sets\nExample:\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nEntity Set\nProduct\nName: Xbox\nCategory: Total Multimedia System\nPrice: $250\nName: My Little Pony Doll\nCategory: Toy\nPrice: $25\nEntity\nEntity Attribute\nEntities are not explicitly represented in E/R diagrams!\n20","title":"Entities vs. Entity Sets","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Keys\nA key is a minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies an entity.\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nDenote elements of the primary key by underlining.\nHere, {name, category} is not a key (it is not minimal).\nIf it were, what would it mean?\nThe E/R model forces us to designate a single primary key, though there may be multiple candidate keys\n21","title":"Keys","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Multivalued and Derived Attributes\nMultivalued Attribute\nDerived Attribute\n22","title":"Multivalued and Derived Attributes","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"The R in E/R: Relationships\nA relationship is between two entities\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nMakes\n23","title":"The R in E/R: ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nA mathematical definition:\nLet A, B be sets\nA={1,2,3},   B={a,b,c,d}\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nA=\nB=\n24","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nA mathematical definition:\nLet A, B be sets\nA={1,2,3},   B={a,b,c,d}\nA x B (the cross-product) is the set of all pairs (a,b)\nA  B = {(1,a), (1,b), (1,c), (1,d), (2,a), (2,b), (2,c), (2,d), (3,a), (3,b), (3,c), (3,d)}\n25\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nA=\nB=","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nA mathematical definition:\nLet A, B be sets\nA={1,2,3},   B={a,b,c,d},\nA x B (the cross-product) is the set of all pairs (a,b)\nA  B = {(1,a), (1,b), (1,c), (1,d), (2,a), (2,b), (2,c), (2,d), (3,a), (3,b), (3,c), (3,d)}\nWe define a relationship to be a subset of A x B\nR = {(1,a), (2,c), (2,d), (3,b)}\n26\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nA=\nB=","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nA mathematical definition:\nLet A, B be sets\nA x B (the cross-product) is the set of all pairs\nA relationship is a subset of A x B\nMakes is relationship- it is a subset of Product  Company:\n27\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nA=\nB=\nmakes\nCompany\nProduct","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\n28\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\n29\nname\ncategory\nprice\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nname\nGizmoWorks\nGadgetCorp\nProduct\nCompany\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\n30\nname\ncategory\nprice\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nname\nGizmoWorks\nGadgetCorp\nProduct\nCompany\nC.name\nP.name\nP.category\nP.price\nGizmoWorks\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoWorks\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGizmoWorks\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nGadgetCorp\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGadgetCorp\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadgetCorp\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nCompany C  Product P\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\n31\nname\ncategory\nprice\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nname\nGizmoWorks\nGadgetCorp\nProduct\nCompany\nC.name\nP.name\nP.category\nP.price\nGizmoWorks\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoWorks\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGizmoWorks\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nGadgetCorp\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGadgetCorp\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadgetCorp\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nCompany C Product P\nC.name\nP.name\nGizmoWorks\nGizmo\nGizmoWorks\nGizmoLite\nGadgetCorp\nGadget\nMakes\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nThere can only be one relationship for every unique combination of entities\nThis also means that the relationship is uniquely determined by the keys of its entities\nExample: the “key” for Makes (to right) is \t{Product.name, Company.name}\n32\nWhy does this make sense?\nThis follows from our mathematical definition of a relationship- it’s a SET!\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nsince\nKeyMakes = KeyProduct     KeyCompany","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Product\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nMakes\nsince\nRelationships may have attributes as well.\nFor example: “since” records when company started making a product\nNote: “since” is implicitly unique per pair here! Why?\nNote #2: Why not “how long”?\nRelationships and Attributes\n33","title":"Product","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nA mathematical definition:\nLet A, B be sets\nA={1,2,3},   B={a,b,c,d}\nA x B (the cross-product) is the set of all pairs (a,b)\nA  B = {(1,a), (1,b), (1,c), (1,d), (2,a), (2,b), (2,c), (2,d), (3,a), (3,b), (3,c), (3,d)}\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nA=\nB=\n34","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nA mathematical definition:\nLet A, B be sets\nA={1,2,3},   B={a,b,c,d},\nA x B (the cross-product) is the set of all pairs (a,b)\nA  B = {(1,a), (1,b), (1,c), (1,d), (2,a), (2,b), (2,c), (2,d), (3,a), (3,b), (3,c), (3,d)}\nWe define a relationship to be a subset of A x B\nR = {(1,a), (2,c), (2,d), (3,b)}\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nA=\nB=\n35","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nA mathematical definition:\nLet A, B be sets\nA x B (the cross-product) is the set of all pairs\nA relationship is a subset of A x B\nMakes is relationship- it is a subset of Product  Company:\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nA=\nB=\nmakes\nCompany\nProduct\n36","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys\n37","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nname\ncategory\nprice\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nname\nGizmoWorks\nGadgetCorp\nProduct\nCompany\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys\n38","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\n39\nname\ncategory\nprice\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nname\nGizmoWorks\nGadgetCorp\nProduct\nCompany\nC.name\nP.name\nP.category\nP.price\nGizmoWorks\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoWorks\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGizmoWorks\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nGadgetCorp\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGadgetCorp\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadgetCorp\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nCompany C  Product P\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nname\ncategory\nprice\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nname\nGizmoWorks\nGadgetCorp\nProduct\nCompany\nC.name\nP.name\nP.category\nP.price\nGizmoWorks\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGizmoWorks\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGizmoWorks\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nGadgetCorp\nGizmo\nElectronics\n$9.99\nGadgetCorp\nGizmoLite\nElectronics\n$7.50\nGadgetCorp\nGadget\nToys\n$5.50\nCompany C Product P\nC.name\nP.name\nGizmoWorks\nGizmo\nGizmoWorks\nGizmoLite\nGadgetCorp\nGadget\nMakes\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nA relationship between entity sets P and C is a subset of all possible pairs of entities in P and C, with tuples uniquely identified by P and C’s keys\n40","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is a Relationship?\nThere can only be one relationship for every unique combination of entities\nThis also means that the relationship is uniquely determined by the keys of its entities\nExample: the “key” for Makes (to right) is \t{Product.name, Company.name}\nWhy does this make sense?\nThis follows from our mathematical definition of a relationship- it’s a SET!\nMakes\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nsince\nKeyMakes = KeyProduct     KeyCompany\n41","title":"What is a Relationship?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Product\nname\ncategory\nprice\nCompany\nname\nMakes\nsince\nRelationships may have attributes as well.\nFor example: “since” records when company started making a product\nNote: “since” is implicitly unique per pair here! Why?\nNote #2: Why not “how long”?\nRelationships and Attributes\n42","title":"Product","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Relationships\nA relationship connects two or more entity sets.\nIt is represented by a diamond, with lines to each of the entity sets  involved.\nThe degree of the relationship defines the number of entity classes  that participate in the relationship\nDegree 1 is a unary relationship\nDegree 2 is a binary relationship\nDegree 3 is a ternary relationship\n43","title":"Relationships","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conceptual Unary Relationship\n44\nPerson\nMarries","title":"Conceptual","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conceptual Binary Relationship\n45\nPerson\nCar\nOwns","title":"Conceptual","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conceptual Unary Relationship\nPerson\nMarries\n46","title":"Conceptual","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conceptual Binary Relationship\nPerson\nCar\nOwns\n47","title":"Conceptual","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Multiplicity of E/R Relationships\n48\nIndicated using arrows\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nOne-to-one:\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nMany-to-one:\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nOne-to-many:\n1\n2\n3\na\nb\nc\nd\nMany-to-many:\nX -> Y means there exists a function mapping from X to Y (recall the definition of a function)","title":"Multiplicity of E/R Relationships","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Minimum Cardinality\nMinimum cardinality is the minimum number of entity instances  that must participate in a relationship instance\nThese values typically assume a value of zero (optional) or one  (mandatory)\n49","title":"Minimum","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Maximum Cardinality\nRelationships are named and classified by their cardinalities, which  is a word that means count (as in the number of items in a set)\nEach of the three types of binary relationship shown previously has  a different maximum cardinality\nMaximum cardinality is the maximum number of entity instances  that can participate in a relationship instance\n– One, many, or some other positive fixed number\n50","title":"Maximum","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conceptual Ternary Relationship\n51\nPatient\nDrug\nPrescription\nDoctor","title":"Conceptual","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"52\nmakes\nbuys\nemploys\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\naddress\nname\nssn\nCompany\nstockprice\nname","title":"makes","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"53\naddress\nname\nssn\nPerson\nbuys\nmakes\nemploys\nCompany\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nstockprice\nname\nprice\nWhat does this say?","title":"33","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?\nWhat about this way?\nNow we can have multiple purchases per product, person pair!\n54\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nname\ndate\nPurchase\nquantity\nPID#\nProductOf\nBuyerOf\nWe can always use a new entity instead of a relationship.  For example, to permit multiple instances of each entity combination!","title":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Cardinality needs two answers\nMinimum participation: may an entity exist without joining the relationship?\nMaximum participation: can it join once, many times, or a fixed number of times?\nAsk from both sides: the same relationship can have different rules for each entity set.\nState the rule in a sentence before drawing notation.\n55","title":"Cardinality needs two answers","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conceptual Ternary Relationship\nPatient\nDrug\nPrescription\nDoctor\n56","title":"Conceptual","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"makes\nbuys\nemploys\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\naddress\nname\nssn\nCompany\nstockprice\nname\n57","title":"makes","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"58\naddress\nname\nssn\nPerson\nbuys\nmakes\nemploys\nCompany\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nstockprice\nname\nprice\nWhat does this say?","title":"33","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?\nWhat about this way?\nNow we can have multiple purchases per product, person pair!\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nname\ndate attribute\nPurchase\nquantity\nPID#\nProductOf\nBuyerOf\nWe can always use a new entity instead of a relationship.  For example, to permit multiple instances of each entity combination!\n59","title":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Multi-way Relationships\n60\nHow do we model a purchase relationship between buyers,\nproducts and stores?\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore","title":"Multi-way Relationships","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships\n61\nQ: What does the arrow mean ?\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\ngiven a person, can determine what they bought and the store where they bought it","title":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships\n62\nQ: What does the arrow mean ?\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\ngiven a store, can determine who shopped there and the product they bought\neach store sells one product and to one person, ever","title":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships\nQ: What does the arrow mean ?\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\ngiven a store, can determine who shopped there and the product they bought\neach store sells one product and to one person, ever\n63","title":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships\nQ: How do we say that every person shops in at most one store ?\nA: Cannot.  This is the best approximation.(Why only approximation ?)\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\nPurchase\n64","title":"Arrows in Multiway Relationships","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Converting Multi-way Relationships to Binary\n65\nPurchase\nPerson\nStore\nProduct\nStoreOf\nProductOf\nBuyerOf\ndate\nFrom what we had on previous slide to this - what did we do?","title":"Converting Multi-way Relationships to Binary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?\nQ: What does this say?\nA: A person can only buy a specific product once (on one date)\n66\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nname\ndate\nModeling something as a relationship makes it unique; what if not appropriate?","title":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Decision: Multi-way or New Entity + Binary?\n67\n(A) Multi-way Relationship\n(B) Entity + Binary\nCovered earlier: (B) is useful if we want to have multiple instances of the “relationship” per entity combination\nMultiple purchases per (product, store, person) combo possible here!\nPurchase\nPerson\nStore\nProduct\nStoreOf\nProductOf\nBuyerOf\ndate\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\n(B) is also useful when we want to add details (constraints or attributes) to the relationship\n“A person who shops in only one store”\n“How long a person has been shopping at a store”\n(A) is useful when a relationship really is between multiple entities\n- \tEx: A three-party legal contract","title":"Decision: Multi-way or New Entity + Binary?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Converting Multi-way Relationships to Binary\nPurchase\nPerson\nStore\nProduct\nStoreOf\nProductOf\nBuyerOf\ndate attribute\nFrom what we had on previous slide to this - what did we do?\n68","title":"Converting Multi-way Relationships to Binary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?\nQ: What does this say?\nA: A person can only buy a specific product once (on one date)\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nname\ndate attribute\nModeling something as a relationship makes it unique; what if not appropriate?\n69","title":"Decision: Relationship vs. Entity?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Decision: Multi-way or New Entity + Binary?\n70\n(A) Multi-way Relationship\n(B) Entity + Binary\nCovered earlier: (B) is useful if we want to have multiple instances of the “relationship” per entity combination\nMultiple purchases per (product, store, person) combo possible here!\nPurchase\nPerson\nStore\nProduct\nStoreOf\nProductOf\nBuyerOf\ndate attribute\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\n(B) is also useful when we want to add details (constraints or attributes) to the relationship\n“A person who shops in only one store”\n“How long a person has been shopping at a store”\n(A) is useful when a relationship really is between multiple entities\n- \tEx: A three-party legal contract","title":"Decision: Multi-way or New Entity + Binary?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Choose an entity when the event has identity\nUse a relationship: the fact is unique for one combination of participating entities.\nUse an associative entity: the event can repeat, needs its own key, or carries a lifecycle.\nTypical clues: quantity, status, timestamp, payment, or line number.\nExample: Purchase is usually an entity when a buyer can purchase the same product repeatedly.\n71","title":"Choose an entity when the event has identity","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Design Principles\n72\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nWhat’s wrong with these examples?\nPresident\nPerson\nCountry","title":"3. Design Principles","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Principles:What’s Wrong?\n73\nPurchase\nProduct\nStore\ndate\npersonName\npersonAddr","title":"Design Principles:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Principles:What’s Wrong?\n74\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\ndate\nDates","title":"Design Principles:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute\n75\nShould address (A) be an attribute?\nEmployee\nAddr 1\nAddr 2\nAddress\nStreet Addr\nZIP\nEmployee\nAddrOf\nOr (B) be an entity?","title":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute\n76\nShould address (A) be an attribute?\nEmployee\nAddr 1\nAddr 2\nHow do we handle employees with multiple addresses here?\nHow do we handle addresses where internal structure of the address (e.g. zip code, state) is useful?","title":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute\n77\nAddress\nStreet Addr\nZIP\nEmployee\nAddrOf\nOr (B) be an entity?\nIn general, when we want to record several values, we choose new entity\nShould address (A) be an attribute?\nEmployee\nAddr 1\nAddr 2","title":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n78","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n79","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Teams play each  other in Games.\nEach pair of teams  can play each  other multiple  times\nPlayers belong  to Teams\nA Play will contain  either a Pass from  one player to  another, or a Run by  one player\nUse the following simplified model of a football season\n(concepts to include are underlined):\nA Game is made up  of Plays that result  in a yardage  gain/loss, and  potentially a  touchdown\nDRAW AN E/R DIAGRAM FOR FOOTBALL\n80","title":"Teams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Games\ndate\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nTeams play each  other in Games.  Each pair of teams  can play each other  multiple times\nPlayingAway\nNote two copies of  the Teams entity  here!\n81","title":"Games","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Players\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nPlayers belong to  Teams (assume no  trades / changes)\nGames\ndate\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\n82","title":"Players","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Game is made up of  Plays that result in a  yardage gain/loss, and  potentially a touchdown\nPlays\nPlay ID\nOccurIn\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nGames\ndate\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\n83","title":"A ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nA Play will contain either  a Pass from one player to  another, or a Run by one  player\nPlays\nPlay ID\nOccurIn\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nGames\ndate\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\nParticipation Type\n84","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ENHANCE YOUR E/R DIAGRAM!\n85\nA player can only  belong to one  team, a play can  only be in one  game, a  pass/run..?\nPlayers can achieve  a Personal Record  linked to a specific  Game and Play\nAlso make sure to add (new concepts underlined):","title":"ENHANCE","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"86\nPlayers can achieve  a Personal Record  linked to a specific  Game and Play","title":"55","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nPlays\nPlay ID\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nTeams\nTeam ID\nParticipation Type\nOccurIn\nGames\ndate\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\nPlayers can achieve  a Personal Record  linked to a specific  Game and Play\nPRIn\n87","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping natural language\n88\nChen proposed the following \"rules of thumb\" for mapping natural  language descriptions into ER diagrams: \"English, Chinese and ER  diagrams\" by Peter Chen.\n•\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entity%E2%80%93relationship_model#Mapping_natural_language","title":"Mapping","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nPlays\nPlay ID\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nTeams\nTeam ID\nParticipation Type\nA player can only  belong to one  team, a play can  only be in one  game, a  pass/run..?\nOccurIn\nGames\ndate\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\n89","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Principles:What’s Wrong?\n90\nPurchase\nProduct\nStore\ndate attribute\npersonName\npersonAddr","title":"Design Principles:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design Principles:What’s Wrong?\n91\nPurchase\nProduct\nPerson\nStore\ndate attribute\nDates","title":"Design Principles:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute\n92\nShould address (A) be an attribute?\nEmployee\nAddr 1\nAddr 2\nAddress\nStreet Addr\nZIP\nEmployee\nAddrOf\nOr (B) be an entity?","title":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute\n93\nShould address (A) be an attribute?\nEmployee\nAddr 1\nAddr 2\nHow do we handle employees with multiple addresses here?\nHow do we handle addresses where internal structure of the address (e.g. zip code, state) is useful?","title":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute\n94\nAddress\nStreet Addr\nZIP\nEmployee\nAddrOf\nOr (B) be an entity?\nIn general, when we want to record several values, we choose new entity\nShould address (A) be an attribute?\nEmployee\nAddr 1\nAddr 2","title":"Examples: Entity vs. Attribute","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n95","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n96","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Teams play each  other in Games.\nEach pair of teams  can play each  other multiple  times\nPlayers belong  to Teams\nA Play will contain  either a Pass from  one player to  another, or a Run by  one player\nUse the following simplified model of a football season\n(concepts to include are underlined):\nA Game is made up  of Plays that result  in a yardage  gain/loss, and  potentially a  touchdown\nDRAW AN E/R DIAGRAM FOR FOOTBALL\n97","title":"Teams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Games\ndate attribute\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nTeams play each  other in Games.  Each pair of teams  can play each other  multiple times\nPlayingAway\nNote two copies of  the Teams entity  here!\n98","title":"Games","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Players\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nPlayers belong to  Teams (assume no  trades / changes)\nGames\ndate attribute\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\n99","title":"Players","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Game is made up of  Plays that result in a  yardage gain/loss, and  potentially a touchdown\nPlays\nPlay ID\nOccurIn\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nGames\ndate attribute\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\n100","title":"A ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nA Play will contain either  a Pass from one player to  another, or a Run by one  player\nPlays\nPlay ID\nOccurIn\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nGames\ndate attribute\nTeams\nTeam ID\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\nParticipation Type\n101","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ENHANCE YOUR E/R DIAGRAM!\nA player can only  belong to one  team, a play can  only be in one  game, a  pass/run..?\nPlayers can achieve  a Personal Record  linked to a specific  Game and Play\nAlso make sure to add (new concepts underlined):\n102","title":"ENHANCE","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"103\nPlayers can achieve  a Personal Record  linked to a specific  Game and Play","title":"55","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nPlays\nPlay ID\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nTeams\nTeam ID\nParticipation Type\nOccurIn\nGames\ndate attribute\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\nPlayers can achieve  a Personal Record  linked to a specific  Game and Play\nPRIn\n104","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping natural language\nChen proposed the following \"rules of thumb\" for mapping natural  language descriptions into ER diagrams: \"English, Chinese and ER  diagrams\" by Peter Chen.\n•\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entity%E2%80%93relationship_model#Mapping_natural_language\n105","title":"Mapping","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"E/R Summary\n106\nE/R diagrams are a visual syntax that allows technical and non-technical people to talk\nFor conceptual design\nBasic constructs: entity, relationship, and attributes\nA good design is faithful to the constraints of the application, but not overzealous\nSummary","title":"E/R Summary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Checkpoint: explain the diagram in plain English\nName each entity set and its key.\nRead every relationship in both directions.\nState the minimum and maximum participation on both sides.\nIdentify one rule the diagram cannot express and document it separately.\n107","title":"Checkpoint: explain the diagram in plain English","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nKey concept:\nBoth Entity sets and Relationships become relations (tables in RDBMS)\n108","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nprice\ncategory\nGizmo1\n99.99\nCamera\nGizmo2\n19.99\nEdible\n109\nProduct\nprice\ncategory\nname\nProduct\nAn entity set becomes table\nEach tuple is one entity\nEach tuple is composed of the entity’s attributes, and has the same primary key","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nprice\ncategory\nGizmo1\n99.99\nCamera\nGizmo2\n19.99\nEdible\n110\nProduct\nprice\ncategory\nname\nProduct\nCREATE TABLE Product(\nname     CHAR(50) PRIMARY KEY,\nprice    DOUBLE,\ncategory VARCHAR(30)\n)","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nfirstname\nlastname\ndate\nGizmo1\nBob\nJoe\n01/01/15\nGizmo2\nJoe\nBob\n01/03/15\nGizmo1\nJoeBob\nSmith\n01/05/15\n111\nPurchased\nA relation between entity sets A1, …, AN also becomes a multiset of tuples / a table\nEach row/tuple is one relation, i.e. one unique combination of entities (a1,…,aN)\nEach row/tuple is\ncomposed of the union of the entity sets’ keys\nhas the entities’ primary keys as foreign keys\nhas the union of the entity sets’ keys as primary key\nFor binary One-to-one and One-to-many there are some special cases.\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate\nlastname","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nfirstname\nlastname\ndate\nGizmo1\nBob\nJoe\n01/01/15\nGizmo2\nJoe\nBob\n01/03/15\nGizmo1\nJoeBob\nSmith\n01/05/15\n112\nPurchased\nA relation between entity sets A1, …, AN also becomes a multiset of tuples / a table\nEach row/tuple is one relation, i.e. one unique combination of entities (a1,…,aN)\nEach row/tuple is\ncomposed of the union of the entity sets’ keys\nhas the entities’ primary keys as foreign keys\nhas the union of the entity sets’ keys as primary key\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate\nlastname","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nfirstname\nlastname\ndate\nGizmo1\nBob\nJoe\n01/01/15\nGizmo2\nJoe\nBob\n01/03/15\nGizmo1\nJoeBob\nSmith\n01/05/15\n113\nPurchased\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate\nlastname\nCREATE TABLE Purchased(\nname      CHAR(50),\nfirstname CHAR(50),\nlastname  CHAR(50),\ndate      DATE,\nPRIMARY KEY (name, firstname, lastname),\nFOREIGN KEY (name)\nREFERENCES Product,\nFOREIGN KEY (firstname, lastname)\nREFERENCES Person\n)","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagram to Relational Schema\nCREATE TABLE purchased (\nFirstname varchar(20),\nLastname varchar(20),\nPname varchar(20),\nSname varchar(20),\npdate  date,\nPrimary key (firstname, lastname, pname,sname),\nForeign key (firstname, lastname) references Person,\nForeign key (pname) references product(name),\nForeign key (sname) references store(name)\n);\n114\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate\nlastname\nStore\nname\naddress\nHow do we represent this as a relational schema?","title":"From E/R Diagram to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"115\nCreate statements","title":"66","width":1440,"height":855,"links":["https://gist.github.com/oldwestbury/bbc6e38eecc098a485ab402f6c666558"]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagram to Relational Schema\nCREATE TABLE purchased (\nFirstname varchar(20),\nLastname varchar(20),\nPname varchar(20),\nSname varchar(20),\npdate  date,\nPrimary key (firstname, lastname, pname,sname),\nForeign key (firstname, lastname) references Person,\nForeign key pname references product(name),\nForeign key sname references store(name)\n);\n116\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate\nlastname\nStore\nname\naddress\nHow do we represent this as a relational schema?","title":"From E/R Diagram to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"117\nerd.sql","title":"67","width":1440,"height":855,"links":["https://oldwestburyedu-my.sharepoint.com/:u:/g/personal/khalefam_oldwestbury_edu/EXfDXUfl_0tIrq2_Qzo48m0Bqj4ou2pLqbRaqTt8Hg6TBA?e=sNkipb"]},{"hidden":true,"text":"118","title":"68","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nprice\ncategory\nGizmo1\n99.99\nCamera\nGizmo2\n19.99\nEdible\n119\nProduct\nprice\ncategory\nname\nProduct\nAn entity set becomes table\nEach tuple is one entity\nEach tuple is composed of the entity’s attributes, and has the same primary key","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nprice\ncategory\nGizmo1\n99.99\nCamera\nGizmo2\n19.99\nEdible\n120\nProduct\nprice\ncategory\nname\nProduct\nCREATE TABLE Product(\nname     CHAR(50) PRIMARY KEY,\nprice    DOUBLE,\ncategory VARCHAR(30)\n)","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nfirstname\nlastname\ndate\nGizmo1\nBob\nJoe\n01/01/15\nGizmo2\nJoe\nBob\n01/03/15\nGizmo1\nJoeBob\nSmith\n01/05/15\n121\nPurchased\nA relation between entity sets A1, …, AN also becomes a multiset of tuples / a table\nEach row/tuple is one relation, i.e. one unique combination of entities (a1,…,aN)\nEach row/tuple is\ncomposed of the union of the entity sets’ keys\nhas the entities’ primary keys as foreign keys\nhas the union of the entity sets’ keys as primary key\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate attribute\nlastname","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema\nname\nfirstname\nlastname\ndate\nGizmo1\nBob\nJoe\n01/01/15\nGizmo2\nJoe\nBob\n01/03/15\nGizmo1\nJoeBob\nSmith\n01/05/15\n122\nPurchased\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate attribute\nlastname\nCREATE TABLE Purchased(\nname      CHAR(50),\nfirstname CHAR(50),\nlastname  CHAR(50),\ndate      DATE,\nPRIMARY KEY (name, firstname, lastname),\nFOREIGN KEY (name)\nREFERENCES Product,\nFOREIGN KEY (firstname, lastname)\nREFERENCES Person\n)","title":"From E/R Diagrams to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"From E/R Diagram to Relational Schema\n123\nPurchased\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nPerson\nfirstname\ndate attribute\nlastname\nStore\nname\naddress\nHow do we represent this as a relational schema?\nCREATE TABLE purchased (\nFirstname varchar(20),\nLastname varchar(20),\nPname varchar(20),\nSname varchar(20),\npdate  date,\nPrimary key (firstname, lastname, pname,sname),\nForeign key (firstname, lastname) references Person,\nForeign key pname references product(name),\nForeign key sname references store(name)\n);","title":"From E/R Diagram to Relational Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"124","title":"67","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"125","title":"68","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"It has SQL for the previous example\n126","title":"It has SQL for the previous example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm\nCOMPANY database example\nAssume that the mapping will create tables with simple single-valued attributes\nStep 1: Mapping of Regular Entity Types\nFor each regular entity type, create a relation R that includes all the simple attributes of E\nCalled entity relations\nEach tuple represents an entity instance\n127","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\n128","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\n129","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\nStep 3: Mapping of Binary 1:1 Relationship Types\nFor each binary 1:1 relationship type\nIdentify relations that correspond to entity types participating in R\nPossible approaches:\nForeign key approach\nMerged relationship approach\nCrossreference or relationship relation approach\nAlternative approach (Avoid this)\nUse the relationship relation (cross-reference) option as in the third option for binary 1:1 relationships\n130","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\nStep 4: Mapping of Binary 1:N Relationship Types\nFor each regular binary 1:N relationship type\nIdentify relation that represents participating entity type at N-side of relationship type\nInclude primary key of other entity type as foreign key in S\nInclude simple attributes of 1:N relationship type as attributes of S\n131","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\nStep 5: Mapping of Binary M:N Relationship Types\nFor each binary M:N relationship type\nCreate a new relation S\nInclude primary key of participating entity types as foreign key attributes in S\nInclude any simple attributes of M:N relationship type\n132","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\nStep 6: Mapping of Multivalued Attributes\nFor each multivalued attribute\nCreate a new relation\nPrimary key of R is the combination of A and K\nIf the multivalued attribute is composite, include its simple components\n133","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\nStep 7: Mapping of N-ary Relationship Types\nFor each n-ary relationship type R\nCreate a new relation S to represent R\nInclude primary keys of participating entity types as foreign keys\nInclude any simple attributes as attributes\n134","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Discussion and Summary of Mapping for ER Model Constructs\n135","title":"Discussion and Summary of Mapping for ER Model Constructs","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"136","title":"78","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping Summary\nEntity set -> table\nSingle attribute  table attribute (column)\nMultivalued Attribute-> table (with the primary key of the entity set and the attribute)\nDerived Attributes  nothing\nMany to many binary (or unary) relationship  table\n1-1 or 1-many binary (or unary) relationship -> attribute to the one side\n3-way or more relationship  table\nTotal participation  NOT NULL (If possible)\n137","title":"Mapping Summary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Discussion and Summary of Mapping for ER Model Constructs\n138","title":"Discussion and Summary of Mapping for ER Model Constructs","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"139","title":"78","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping Summary\nEntity set -> table\nSingle attribute  table attribute (column)\nMultivalued Attribute-> table (with the primary key of the entity set and the attribute)\nDerived Attributes  nothing\nMany to many binary (or unary) relationship  table\n1-1 or 1-many binary (or unary) relationship -> attribute to the one side\n3-way or more relationship  table\nTotal participation  NOT NULL\n140","title":"Mapping Summary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example in Class\n141\nDept\nEmpl\nmanages","title":"Example in Class","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example in Class\nCREATE TABLE Dept(\nname  char(10) ,\nbudget decimal(10,2),\nmanagerSSN char(10) NOT NULL /*Total Particpation*/,\nforeign key managerSSN references Person(ssn),\nmanagerstartdate date\nPrimary Key(name)\n);\n142","title":"Example in Class","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping decisions you should make automatically\nEntity set -> table; key attribute -> primary key.\n1:N relationship -> foreign key on the N side.\nM:N or n-ary relationship -> new table containing participant keys.\nMultivalued attribute -> new table; derived attribute -> usually do not store.\n143","title":"Mapping decisions you should make automatically","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example in Class\nDept\nEmpl\nmanages\n144","title":"Example in Class","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example in Class\nCREATE TABLE Dept(\nname  char(10) ,\nbudget decimal(10,2),\nmanagerSSN char(10) NOT NULL /*Total Participation*/,\nFOREIGN KEY (managerSSN) REFERENCES Person(ssn),\nmanager_start_ts TIMESTAMP,\nPRIMARY KEY (name)\n);\n145","title":"Example in Class","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Not Recommended\n/* no longer needed*/ Not Recommended\nCREATE TABLE Manages(\nmanagerSSN char(10),\ndeptname char(10),\nstartdate DATE,\nPrimary key (managerSSN,deptname),\nForeign Key deptname references dept(name),\nForeign Key managerSSN references person(ssn)\n)\n146","title":"Not Recommended","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"CREATE TABLE Person(\nname  varchar(100) not null,\nsalary decimal(10,2),\nSSN char(10),\nunique(name),\nPrimary Key(ssn)\n);\n147","title":"CREATE TABLE Person(","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Advanced E/R Concepts (EERD)\n148","title":"3. Advanced E/R Concepts (EERD)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nSubclasses & connection to OO\nConstraints\nWeak entity sets\n149","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Modeling Subclasses\n150\nSome objects in a class may be special, i.e. worthy of their own class\nDefine a new class?\nBut what if we want to maintain connection to current class?\nBetter: define a subclass\nEx:\nProducts\nSoftware\nproducts\nEducational\nproducts\nWe can define subclasses in E/R!","title":"Modeling Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"151\nProduct\nname\nprice\nSoftware Product\nplatforms\nEducational Product\nageGroup\nisA\nModeling Subclasses\nChild subclasses contain all the attributes of all of their parent classes plus the new attributes shown attached to them in the E/R diagram","title":"87","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Understanding Subclasses\nThink in terms of records; ex:\nProduct\nSoftwareProduct\nEducationalProduct\n152\nname\nprice\nname\nprice\nplatforms\nname\nprice\nageGroup\nChild subclasses contain all the attributes of all of their parent classes plus the new attributes shown attached to them in the E/R diagram\nProduct\nname\nprice\nisA\nEducational Product\nSoftware Product\nageGroup\nplatforms","title":"Understanding Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Think like tables…\n153\nProduct\nname\nprice\nisA\nEducational Product\nSoftware Product\nageGroup\nplatforms\nname\nprice\nGizmo\n99\nCamera\n49\nToy\n39\nname\nplatforms\nGizmo\nunix\nname\nageGroup\nGizmo\ntoddler\nToy\nretired\nProduct\nSw.Product\nEd.Product","title":"Think like tables…","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"154\nProduct\nname\nprice\nSoftware Product\nplatforms\nEducational Product\nageGroup\nisA\nModeling Subclasses\nChild subclasses contain all the attributes of all of their parent classes plus the new attributes shown attached to them in the E/R diagram","title":"87","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Understanding Subclasses\nThink in terms of records; ex:\nProduct\nSoftwareProduct\nEducationalProduct\n155\nname\nprice\nname\nprice\nplatforms\nname\nprice\nageGroup\nChild subclasses contain all the attributes of all of their parent classes plus the new attributes shown attached to them in the E/R diagram\nProduct\nname\nprice\nisA\nEducational Product\nSoftware Product\nageGroup\nplatforms","title":"Understanding Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Think like tables…\n156\nProduct\nname\nprice\nisA\nEducational Product\nSoftware Product\nageGroup\nplatforms\nname\nprice\nGizmo\n99\nCamera\n49\nToy\n39\nname\nplatforms\nGizmo\nunix\nname\nageGroup\nGizmo\ntoddler\nToy\nretired\nProduct\nSw.Product\nEd.Product","title":"Think like tables…","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"IsA Review\nIf we declare A IsA B then every A is a B\nWe use IsA to\nAdd descriptive attributes to a subclass\nTo identify entities that participate in a relationship\nNo need for multiple inheritance\n157","title":"IsA","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Modeling UnionTypes With Subclasses\n158\nSuppose each piece of furniture is owned either by a person, or by a company.  How do we represent this?\nFurniturePiece\nPerson\nCompany","title":"Modeling UnionTypes With Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses\nSay: each piece of furniture is owned either by a person, or by a company\nSolution 1. Acceptable, but imperfect (What’s wrong ?)\n159\nFurniturePiece\nPerson\nCompany\nownedByPerson\nownedByComp","title":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses\nSolution 2: better (though more laborious)\n160\nCompany\nFurniturePiece\nPerson\nownedBy\nOwner\nisa\nWhat is happening here?","title":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses\nSay: each piece of furniture is owned either by a person, or by a company\nSolution 1. Acceptable, but imperfect (What’s wrong ?)\n161\nFurniturePiece\nPerson\nCompany\nownedByPerson\nownedByComp","title":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses\nSolution 2: better (though more laborious)\n162\nCompany\nFurniturePiece\nPerson\nownedBy\nOwner\nisa\nWhat is happening here?","title":"Modeling Union Types with Subclasses","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Constraints in E/R Diagrams\n163\nFinding constraints is part of the E/R modeling process. Commonly used constraints are:\nKeys: Implicit constraints on uniqueness of entities\nEx: An SSN uniquely identifies a person\nSingle-value constraints:\nEx: a person can have only one father\nReferential integrity constraints: Referenced entities must exist\nEx: if you work for a company, it must exist in the database\nOther constraints:\nEx: peoples’ ages are between 0 and 150\nRecall FOREIGN KEYs!","title":"Constraints in E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Participation Constraints: Partial v. Total\n164\nmakes\nProduct\nCompany\nAre there products made by no company? Companies that don’t make a product?\nmakes\nProduct\nCompany\nBold line indicates total participation (i.e. here: all products are made by a company)","title":"Participation Constraints: Partial v. Total","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Participation Constraints: Partial v. Total\n165\nmakes\nProduct\nCompany\nAre there products made by no company? Companies that don’t make a product?\nmakes\nProduct\nCompany\nBold line indicates total participation (i.e. here: all products are made by a company)","title":"Participation Constraints: Partial v. Total","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Keys in E/R Diagrams\n166\naddress\nname\nssn\nPerson\nProduct\nname\ncategory\nprice\nUnderline keys:\nNote: no formal way to specify multiple keys in E/R diagrams…","title":" Keys in E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Single Value Constraints\n167\nmakes\nmakes\nv. s.\nSee previous section!","title":"Single Value Constraints","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Referential Integrity Constraints\n168\nCompany\nProduct\nmakes\nCompany\nProduct\nmakes\nEach product made by at most one company.\nSome products made by no company?\nEach product made by exactly one company.","title":"Referential Integrity Constraints","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Weak Entity Sets\n169\nEntity sets are weak when their key comes from other\nclasses to which they are related.\n“Football team” v. “The Stanford Football team” (E.g., Berkeley has a football team too, sort of)\nname\nUniversity\nTeam\naffiliation\nnumber\nsport","title":"Weak Entity Sets","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Weak Entity Sets\n170\nEntity sets are weak when their key comes from other\nclasses to which they are related.\nUniversity\nTeam\naffiliation\nnumber\nsport\nname\nnumber is a partial key. (denote with dashed underline).\nUniversity is called the identifying owner.\nParticipation in affiliation must be total. Why?","title":"Weak Entity Sets","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strong and Weak Entities\nA weak entity is an entity whose instances cannot exist in the  database without the existence of an instance of another entity\nAny entity that is not weak entity is called a strong entity\n– Instances of a strong entity can exist in the database independently\nThe weak entity’s identifier is a combination of the identifier of the  owner entity and the partial key of the weak entity.\n171","title":"Strong","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n172","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n173","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"174\nPlayers have a weight which changes in on vs. off-season","title":"Players have a ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nPlays\nPlay ID\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nTeams\nTeam ID\nParticipation Type\nOccurIn\nGames\ndate\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\nPRIn\nPlayers might  have different  weights at  different times\nNote: point here is that  different players might  have different numbers of  training / weight phases-  hence should represent as  new entity!\nWeightOf\nWeight\nWeight\nTime Period\n175","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ADD IN: SUBCLASSES, CONSTRAINTS,  AND WEAK ENTITY SETS\n176\nTeams belong to  cities- model as  weak entity sets\nPlayers are either  on Offense or  Defense, and are  of types (QB, RB,  WR, TE, K)\nAll passes are  to exactly one  player; all runs  include a  player\nConcepts to include / model:\nMake sure you  have designated  keys for all our  concepts!","title":"ADD IN: SUBCLASSES, CONSTRAINTS, ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"LocatedIn\nCities\nTeam Name\nTeams\nTeams belong to  cities- model as  weak entity sets\nName\n177","title":"LocatedIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Teams\nLocatedIn\nCities\nTeam Name\nName\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nPlaysPosition\nOffensePosition           DefensePosition\nPosition\nisa\nPlayers are either  on Offense or  Defense, and are  of types (QB, RB,  WR, TE, etc.)\nString\nType\nOffensivePosType\nDefensivePosType\n178","title":"T","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Weak entities borrow identity - not just existence\nThe owner contributes part of the weak entity's key.\nThe partial key distinguishes weak entities under the same owner.\nTotal participation means every weak entity must have an owner.\nMapping rule: combine the owner's key with the partial key as the weak table's primary key.\n179","title":"Weak entities borrow identity - not just existence","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n180","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams\n181","title":"ACTIVITY: E/R Diagrams","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Players have a weight which changes in on vs. off-season\n182","title":"Players have a ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nPlays\nPlay ID\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nTeams\nTeam ID\nParticipation Type\nOccurIn\nGames\ndate attribute\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\nPRIn\nPlayers might  have different  weights at  different times\nNote: point here is that  different players might  have different numbers of  training / weight phases-  hence should represent as  new entity!\nWeightOf\nWeight\nWeight\nTime Period\n183","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ADD IN: SUBCLASSES, CONSTRAINTS,  AND WEAK ENTITY SETS\nTeams belong to  cities- model as  weak entity sets\nPlayers are either  on Offense or  Defense, and are  of types (QB, RB,  WR, TE, K)\nAll passes are  to exactly one  player; all runs  include a  player\nConcepts to include / model:\nMake sure you  have designated  keys for all our  concepts!\n184","title":"ADD IN: SUBCLASSES, CONSTRAINTS, ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"LocatedIn\nCities\nTeam Name\nTeams\nTeams belong to  cities- model as  weak entity sets\nName\n185","title":"LocatedIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Teams\nLocatedIn\nCities\nTeam Name\nName\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nPlaysPosition\nOffensePosition           DefensePosition\nPosition\nisa\nPlayers are either  on Offense or  Defense, and are  of types (QB, RB,  WR, TE, etc.)\nString\nType\nOffensivePosType\nDefensivePosType\n186","title":"T","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)\nStep 2: Mapping of Weak Entity Types\nFor each weak entity type, create a relation R and include all simple attributes of the entity type as attributes of R\nInclude primary key attribute of owner as foreign key attributes of R\n187","title":"ER-to-Relational Mapping Algorithm (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"188\nname\nWeightOf\nWeight\nWeightinlbs\nTime Period\nPlayer\nPlayer ID\nWeight(Player_id, time_period, weightinlbs)\nPlayer_id is a foreign key into Player","title":"107","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"189\nname\nWeightOf\nWeight\nWeightinlbs\nTime Period\nPlayer\nPlayer ID\nWeight(Player_id, time_period, weightinlbs)","title":"109","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"190\nname\nWeightOf\nWeight\nWeightinlbs\nTime Period\nPlayer\nPlayer ID\nWeight(Player_id, time_period, weightinlbs)","title":"109","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping EER Model Constructs to Relations\nExtending ER-to-relational mapping algorithm\n191","title":"Mapping EER Model Constructs to Relations","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization\nStep 8: Options for Mapping Specialization or Generalization\nOption 8A: Multiple relations—superclass and subclasses\nFor any specialization (total or partial, disjoint or overlapping)\nPerson(ssn, name)\nEmployee(ssn, salary)\nStudent(ssn, major)\nAlumnus(ssn)\nOption 8B: Multiple relations—subclass relations only\nSubclasses are total\nSpecialization has disjointedness constraint\nEmployee(ssn, name,salary)\nStudent(ssn, name,major)\nAlumnus(ssn,name)\n192\nPerson\nIsA\nEmployee\nAlumnus\nStudent\nSSN\nmajor\nsalary\nName","title":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization (cont’d.)\nOption 8C: Single relation with one type attribute\nType or discriminating attribute indicates subclass of tuple\nSubclasses are disjoint\nPotential for generating many NULL values if many specific attributes exist in the subclasses\nPerson(ssn, name, type, salary, major)\nOption 8D: Single relation with multiple type attributes\nSubclasses are overlapping\nWill also work for a disjoint specialization\nPerson(ssn, name, isEmployee, isStudent, isAlumnus, salary, major)\n193\nPerson\nIsA\nEmployee\nAlumnus\nStudent\nSSN\nmajor\nsalary\nName","title":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization\nStep 8: Options for Mapping Specialization or Generalization\nOption 8A: Multiple relations—superclass and subclasses\nFor any specialization (total or partial, disjoint or overlapping)\nPerson(ssn, name)\nEmployee(ssn, salary)\nStudent(ssn, major)\nAlumnus(ssn)\nOption 8B: Multiple relations—subclass relations only\nSubclasses are total\nSpecialization has disjointedness constraint\nEmployee(ssn, name,salary)\nStudent(ssn, name,major)\nAlumnus(ssn,name)\nPerson\nIsA\nEmployee\nAlumnus\nStudent\nSSN\nmajor\nsalary\nName\n194","title":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization (cont’d.)\nOption 8C: Single relation with one type attribute\nType or discriminating attribute indicates subclass of tuple\nSubclasses are disjoint\nPotential for generating many NULL values if many specific attributes exist in the subclasses\nPerson(ssn, name, type, salary, major)\nOption 8D: Single relation with multiple type attributes\nSubclasses are overlapping\nWill also work for a disjoint specialization\nPerson(ssn, name, isEmployee, isStudent, isAlumnus, salary, major)\nPerson\nIsA\nEmployee\nAlumnus\nStudent\nSSN\nmajor\nsalary\nName\n195","title":"Mapping of Specialization or Generalization (cont’d.)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping of Shared Subclasses (Multiple Inheritance)\nApply any of the options discussed in step 8 to a shared subclass\n196","title":"Mapping of Shared Subclasses (Multiple Inheritance)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Mapping of Categories (Union Types)\nStep 9: Mapping of Union Types (Categories)\nDefining superclasses have different keys\nSpecify a new key attribute\nSurrogate key\n197","title":"Mapping of Categories (Union Types)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Person\nBank\nCompany\nOwner\nisA\ncname\nbname\nssn\nRegistered_vehicle\ncar\ntruck\nVehicle_id\nPlate number\nisA\nowns\nPurchase date\nstyle\ntonnage\nOwner_id\n198\nOwner(  owner_id)\nCompany (cname, owner_id)\nBank (bname, owner_id)\nPerson (ssn, owner_id)\nRVehicle(vehicle_id, platenumber)\nCar ( vehicle_id, style)\nTruck (vehicle_id, tonnage)\nOWNS( vehicle_id, owner_id, purchase_date)","title":"Person","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Chen Notation (Summary)\n199\nEntity Set\nWeak Entity Set\nRelationship\nAttribute\nKey Attribute\nMultivalued Attribute\nDerived Attribute\nE1\nE2\nR\nE1 has a most one in R\nE1\nE2\nR\nEach tuple in E2 must participate in R\n(total participation)","title":"Chen Notation (Summary)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n200\n1 Employee works in 1 department\nEmployee\nDepartment\nworks\n1 Department  have  Many Employees\nEvery employee works for a department","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Examples\n201","title":"ERD Examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Examples\n202","title":"ERD Examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"203","title":"119","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"204","title":"120","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"205","title":"121","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"206","title":"122","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"207","title":"123","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Advanced Notation\n208","title":"Advanced Notation","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Weak Entity in E/R Diagrams\n209\nPlayer Weight\nPlayer\nPlays-  on\nweight\nname\nTime Period\nDouble diamond for supporting many-one relationship with Weak Entity.\nDouble rectangle for the weak entity set.","title":"Weak Entity in","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Problems\n210","title":"ERD Problems","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Problems\n211","title":"ERD Problems","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Person\nBank\nCompany\nOwner\nisA\ncname\nbname\nssn\nRegistered_vehicle\ncar\ntruck\nVehicle_id\nPlate number\nisA\nowns\nPurchase date\nstyle\ntonnage\nOwner_id\n212\nOwner(  owner_id)\nCompany (cname, owner_id)\nBank (bname, owner_id)\nPerson (ssn, owner_id)\nRVehicle(vehicle_id, platenumber)\nCar ( vehicle_id, style)\nTruck (vehicle_id, tonnage)\nOWNS( vehicle_id, owner_id, purchase_date)","title":"Person","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Chen Notation (Summary)\n213\nEntity Set\nWeak Entity Set\nRelationship\nAttribute\nKey Attribute\nMultivalued Attribute\nDerived Attribute\nE1\nE2\nR\nE1 has a most one in R\nE1\nE2\nR\nEach tuple in E2 must participate in R\n(total participation)","title":"Chen Notation (Summary)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\n214\n1 Employee works in 1 department\nEmployee\nDepartment\nworks\n1 Department  have  Many Employees\nEvery employee works for a department","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Examples\n215","title":"ERD Examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Examples\n216","title":"ERD Examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"217","title":"119","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"218","title":"120","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"219","title":"121","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"220","title":"122","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"221","title":"123","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Advanced Notation\n222","title":"Advanced Notation","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Weak Entity in E/R Diagrams\nPlayer Weight\nPlayer\nPlays-  on\nweight\nname\nTime Period\nDouble diamond for supporting many-one relationship with Weak Entity.\nDouble rectangle for the weak entity set.\n223","title":"Weak Entity in","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Problems\n224","title":"ERD Problems","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Problems\n225","title":"ERD Problems","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Congress\nDesign an ER schema for keeping track of information about votes taken in the U.S. House of Representatives during the current two-year congressional session.\nThe database needs to keep track of each U.S. STATEs Name (e.g., Texas, New York, California) and include the Region of the state(whose domain is Northeast, Midwest, Southeast, Southwest, West).\nEach CONGRESSPERSON in the House of Representatives is described by his or her Name, plus the District represented, the Start date when the congressperson was first elected, and the political Party to which he or she belongs (whose domain is Republican, Democrat, Independent, Other).\nThe database keeps track of each BILL (i.e., proposed law), including the Bill name, the Date of vote on the bill, whether the bill Passed or failed (whose domain is Yes, No), and the Sponsor (the congressperson(s) who sponsored that is, proposed the bill).\nThe database also keeps track of how each congressperson voted on each bill (domain of Vote attribute is Yes, No, Abstain, Absent).\nDraw an ER schema diagram for this application. State clearly any assumptions you make\n226","title":"Congress","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"227\nDiagram https://www.tldraw.com/r/12YKkWcscWMT87vOHgE_b?d=v0.0.1920.919.page\nCREATE TABLE statements\nhttps://www.tldraw.com/f/t15jFtJli7Q8_gv-EZ3PD?d=v0.-200.1655.910.page","title":"126","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Problem 2\nYou have just been hired as a consultant by the local airport. Impressed by your background in databases, they want you to completely redesign their database system. Your first task is to organize the information about all the airplanes stationed and maintained at the airport. The relevant information is as follows:\nEvery airplane has a registration number, and each airplane is of a specific model.\nThe airport accommodates a number of airplane models, and each model is identified by a model number (e.g., DC-lO) and has acapacity and a weight.\nA number of technicians work at the airport. You need to store the name, SSN, address, phone number, and salary of each technician\nEach technician is an expert on one or more plane model(s), and his or her expertise may overlap with that of other technicians. This information about technicians must also be recorded.\nTraffic controllers  are union members, must have an annual medical examination. For each traffic controller, you must store the date of the most recent exam.\nAll airport employees (including technicians and traffic controller) belong to a union. You must store the union member-ship number of each employee. You can assume that each employee is uniquely identified by a social security number\nThe airport has a number of tests that are used periodically to ensure that airplanes are still airworthy.Each test has a Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) test number, a name, and a maximum possible score.\nThe FAA requires the airport to keep track of each time a given airplane is tested by a given technician using a given test. For each testing event, the information needed is the date, the number of hours the technician spent doing the test, and the score the airplane received on the test\n(20 points) Draw an ER diagram for the airport database. Be sure to indicate the various attributes of each entity and relationship set; also specify the key and participation constraints for each relationships. Specify any necessary overlap and covering constraints a.s well (in English)(5 points) The FAA passes a regulation that tests on a plane must be conducted by a technician whois an expert on that model. How would you express this constraint in the ER diagram? If you cannot express it, explain briefly.\n228","title":"Problem 2","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Check notes\n229","title":"128","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"230\nDiagram https://www.tldraw.com/r/12YKkWcscWMT87vOHgE_b?d=v0.0.1920.919.page\nhttps://gist.github.com/oldwestbury/554927f8aa1b09c694ea289a252df5c4\nhttps://www.tldraw.com/f/t15jFtJli7Q8_gv-EZ3PD?d=v0.-200.1655.910.page","title":"128","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Problem 2\nYou have just been hired as a consultant by the local airport. Impressed by your background in databases, they want you to completely redesign their database system. Your first task is to organize the information about all the airplanes stationed and maintained at the airport. The relevant information is as follows:\nEvery airplane has a registration number, and each airplane is of a specific model.\nThe airport accommodates a number of airplane models, and each model is identified by a model number (e.g., DC-lO) and has acapacity and a weight.\nA number of technicians work at the airport. You need to store the name, SSN, address, phone number, and salary of each technician\nEach technician is an expert on one or more plane model(s), and his or her expertise may overlap with that of other technicians. This information about technicians must also be recorded.\nTraffic controllers must have an annual medical examination. For each traffic controller, you must store the date of the most recent exam.\nAll airport employees (including technicians) belong to a union. You must store the union member-ship number of each employee. You can assume that each employee is uniquely identified by a social security number\nThe airport has a number of tests that are used periodically to ensure that airplanes are still airworthy.Each test has a Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) test number, a name, and a maximum possible score.\nThe FAA requires the airport to keep track of each time a given airplane is tested by a given technician using a given test. For each testing event, the information needed is the date, the number of hours the technician spent doing the test, and the score the airplane received on the test\n(20 points) Draw an ER diagram for the airport database. Be sure to indicate the various attributes of each entity and relationship set; also specify the key and participation constraints for each relationships. Specify any necessary overlap and covering constraints a.s well (in English)(5 points) The FAA passes a regulation that tests on a plane must be conducted by a technician whois an expert on that model. How would you express this constraint in the ER diagram? If you cannot express it, explain briefly.\n231","title":"Problem 2","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Check notes\n232","title":"130","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"233","title":"131","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Convert to logical Schema\nAirport(airport code, city , state, Name)\nAirplane(airplane_id , total_no_of_seats, type_name)\nAirplane_type(type_name, company, max_seat)\nFLIGHT(flight_number, weekdays, airline)\nCAN_LAND(airport_code, type_name)\nFARE(code, flight_number, amount, restriction)\nFLIGHT_LEG(leg_no, flight_number, dep_airport_code,\nsch_dep_time, arr_airport_code, sch_arr_time)\nLEG_INSTANCE(leg_no, flight_number, date,no_of_avail_seats,airplane_id)\nSEAT(leg_no, flight_number, date,seat_no, customer_name, cphone)\n234","title":"Convert to logical Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Company(ID,  name , address )\nStaff( StaffID , dob , address, companyID, since )\nChild( ChildID , name , StaffID )\nWife ( WifeID , name, StaffID)\nPhone( StaffID, phoneNumber )\nTask ( TaskID , description)\nPerform(StaffID , TaskID )\nPrimary Key is underlined. Foreign key is bold italic\n235","title":"Company(","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Convert to logical Schema\n236","title":"Convert to logical Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"237","title":"133","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"238","title":"135","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"239\nDiagram https://www.tldraw.com/r/12YKkWcscWMT87vOHgE_b?d=v0.0.1920.919.page\nhttps://gist.github.com/oldwestbury/554927f8aa1b09c694ea289a252df5c4\nhttps://www.tldraw.com/f/t15jFtJli7Q8_gv-EZ3PD?d=v0.-200.1655.910.page","title":"128","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Problem 2 — Airport Database\nScenario — Redesign the local airport database for airplanes that are stationed and maintained there.\nAirplane — Store a unique registration number; each airplane has exactly one model.\nModel — Store model number, capacity, and weight; the airport accommodates many models.\nTechnician — Store name, SSN, address, phone, and salary. A technician may be an expert on several models, and expertise can overlap.\nTraffic controller — Record the date of the most recent annual medical examination.\nEmployee — Use SSN as the identifier and record the employee's union membership number.\nTest — Store FAA test number, name, and maximum possible score.\nTesting event — For an airplane tested by a technician using a test, record date, hours spent, and score.\n20 points — Draw the E/R diagram with attributes, keys, participation constraints, and any overlap or covering constraints.\n5-point challenge — A test on a plane must be conducted by a technician who is an expert on that model. Express the constraint in E/R, or explain why it cannot be expressed directly.\n240","title":"Problem 2 — Airport Database","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Check notes\n241","title":"130","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"242\nCheck notes","title":"130","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"243","title":"131","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Convert to a Logical Schema\nAirport(airport_code, city, state, name)\nAirplane(airplane_id, total_no_of_seats, type_name)\nAirplane_type(type_name, company, max_seat)\nFLIGHT(flight_number, weekdays, airline)\nCAN_LAND(airport_code, type_name)\nFARE(code, flight_number, amount, restriction)\nFLIGHT_LEG(leg_no, flight_number,\ndep_airport_code, sch_dep_time, arr_airport_code, sch_arr_time)\nLEG_INSTANCE(leg_no, flight_number, date, no_of_avail_seats, airplane_id)\nSEAT(leg_no, flight_number, date, seat_no, customer_name, cphone)\n244","title":"Convert to a Logical Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Company(ID,  name , address )\nStaff( StaffID , dob , address, companyID, since )\nChild( ChildID , name , StaffID )\nWife ( WifeID , name, StaffID)\nPhone( StaffID, phoneNumber )\nTask ( TaskID , description)\nPerform(StaffID , TaskID )\nPrimary Key is underlined. Foreign key is bold italic\n245","title":"Company(","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Convert to logical Schema\n246","title":"Convert to logical Schema","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"247","title":"135","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"create table bank(code char(10), name varchar(20), addr varchar(100), primary key code)\nCreate table bankbranch (code char(10), bronchno int, addr varchar(100), primary key (code, brachno), foreign key code references bank on delete cascade)\ncreate table loan(loanno int primary key amount decimal (10,2), type char(10) , code char(10) NOT  NULL, branchno int NOT NULL, foreign key code reference bankbranch, foreign key branchno references bankbranch )\ncreate table account(acctno int primary key, balance decimal (10,2), type char(10) , code char(10) , branchno, foreign key code reference bankbrach ,foreign key branchno references bankbranch)\ncreate table customer(ssn char(10) primary key, name varchar(20),phone char(10), addr varchar(100))\ncreate table AC (ssn char(10), actno int, primary key (ssn_actno), foreign key (ssn) references customer, foreign key (act_no) references account)\ncreate table LC (ssn char(10), loanno int, primary key (ssn_actno), foreign key (ssn) references customer, foreign key (loanno) references loan)\n248","title":"create table ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Example\nA retail company keeps tracks of Customer, Order, Line Item, Part, Supplier, Nation.\nFor customer, we keep track of name, id, and market segment.\nCustomer makes  orders.  The systems stores order number, order total price and date of the order.\nEach order can have multiple line items. For each line item, we store quantity, extended price, and return flag.\nEach line item (with in an order is identified by line no) corresponds to a part, we store id for this part,  its   size, and  its retail price. The Supplier supplies these parts. We store supplier_id, name, address and account balance for supplier.\nBoth suppliers and customers belongs to a nation. We store nation name, capital and region.w\n249","title":"ERD Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Employee(\nSSN, fname, lname, minit, bdate, gender, salary, address,\nd_number NOT NULL,\nsupervisor_ssn\nprimary key (SSN)\n)\nDepartment(Number, Name)\nDept_loc(Number, location,\nmanager_ssn NOT NULL\nprimary key (Number, location))\nProject(Number, Name, Location,\ndepartment_number NOT NULL)\nWorks_on(Project_number, ssn, hours\nprimary key (project_number, ssn))\n250","title":"Employee(","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Exit ticket: requirement -> E/R -> relation\nModel: extract entities, relationships, attributes, and constraints from one sentence.\nDefend: explain one cardinality and one entity-versus-attribute choice.\nMap: write the resulting tables with primary and foreign keys.\nAudit: check whether every requirement is represented somewhere.\n251","title":"Exit ticket: requirement -> E/R -> relation","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lab: model a campus event system\nEvents: each has an ID, title, start/end time, one sponsoring department, and one room.\nRegistrations: students register for many events; record registration time and attendance.\nSpeakers: an event may have many speakers; record talk title and speaking order for each assignment.\nBusiness rule: the same room cannot host overlapping events.\n252","title":"Lab: model a campus event system","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lab: model, map, audit\n1. Model: draw entities, keys, attributes, relationships, and min/max participation on both sides.\n2. Defend: explain one cardinality and whether Registration and SpeakingAssignment need associative entities.\n3. Map: write the relations with primary keys, foreign keys, and relationship attributes.\n4. Audit: state how SQL will enforce end > start, unique registration, speaking order, and room-time conflicts.\n253","title":"Lab: model, map, audit","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ParticipateIn\nPlays\nPlay ID\nYardage Diff\nTouchdown\nPlayers\nname\nMemberOf\nPlayer ID\nTeams\nTeam ID\nParticipation Type\nA player can only  belong to one  team, a play can  only be in one  game, a  pass/run..?\nOccurIn\nGames\ndate attribute\nPlayingHome\nlocation\nPlayingAway\n254","title":"ParticipateIn","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Review","title":"ERD Review","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD Example\nSuppose you are given the following requirements for a simple database for the National Hockey League (NHL):\nthe NHL has many teams,\neach team has a name, a city, a coach, a captain, and a set of players,\neach player belongs to only one team,\neach player has a name, a position (such as left wing or goalie), a skill level, and a set of injury records,\na team captain is also a player,\na game is played between two teams (referred to as host_team and guest_team) and has a date (such as May 11th, 2023) and a score (such as 4 to 2).\nConstruct a clean and concise ER diagram for the NHL database using the Chen notation as in your textbook.  List your assumptions and clearly indicate the cardinality mappings as well as any role indicators in your ER diagram.","title":"ERD Example ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"","title":"[visual slide]","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"","title":"[visual slide]","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Important Note","title":"Important Note","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Suppose the bookstore adds music cassettes and compactdisks to its collection.The same music item may be present in cassette or compactdisk format, with differing prices. Extend the E-R diagram to model this addition, ignoring the effect on shopping baskets..Now extend the E-R diagram, using generalization,to model the case where a shopping basket may contain any combination of books, musiccassettes, or compactdisks.","title":"Suppose the bookstore adds music cassettes and ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"","title":"[visual slide]","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"","title":"[visual slide]","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"","title":"[visual slide]","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"","title":"[visual slide]","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"","title":"[visual slide]","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Design an ER schema for keeping track of information about votes taken in the U.S. House of Representatives during the current two-year congressional session. The database needs to keep track of each U.S. STATEs Name (e.g., Texas, New York, California) and include the Region of the state(whose domain is Northeast, Midwest, Southeast, Southwest, West). Each CONGRESSPERSON in the House of Representatives is described by his or her Name, plus the District represented, the Start date when the congressperson was first elected, and the political Party to which he or she belongs (whose domain is Republican, Democrat, Independent, Other). The database keeps track of each BILL (i.e., proposed law), including the Bill name, the Date of vote on the bill, whether the bill Passed or failed (whose domain is Yes, No), and the Sponsor (the congressperson(s) who sponsored that is, proposed the bill). The database also keeps track of how each congressperson voted on each bill (domain of Vote attribute is Yes, No, Abstain, Absent). Draw an ER schema diagram for this application. State clearly any assumptions you make","title":"Design an ER schema for keeping track of information about votes taken in the U.S. House of Representatives during the current two-year congressional session. The database needs to keep track of each U.S. ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERD","title":"ERD","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"268\nPractice and hosted labs\nER modeling and relational mapping\nER modeling and relationships: Lab 4 theory section\nForeign keys and referential integrity\nMulti-table relationships\nYour task: Identify entities and keys, state cardinality in both directions, record assumptions, then map the design to tables and foreign keys.\nHosted on GitHub Pages. Internet access required.","title":"Practice and hosted labs","width":1440,"height":855,"links":["https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/advanced-sql/advanced-sql-techniques.html","https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/joins-foreign-keys/foreign-keys.html","https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/joins-foreign-keys/multi-tables.html"]}];
